  "content ":"<p class="lead-paragraph">"I look forward to meet you." Read that again — it feels almost right, doesn't it? That single word "to" is responsible for more silent errors in SSC CGL and Bank PO papers than almost any other grammar point, because "to" secretly plays two completely different roles in English, and textbooks rarely explain which one you're dealing with until it's too late.</p>

<div style="background:#f0f7ff;border-left:4px solid #1B3A6B;padding:15px 18px;margin:20px 0;border-radius:4px;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>Quick Answer:</strong> "To" works as an infinitive marker (to + base verb, like "to go") in most sentences, but after certain fixed expressions — look forward to, object to, used to (habit), be accustomed to — "to" is actually a preposition, which means it must be followed by a gerund (-ing form), never the base verb.</p>
</div>

