<h2 id="the-problem">Why the "To" Trap Feels Harder Than It Is</h2>
<p>Every student learns "to + verb" as the infinitive form early on — to go, to eat, to study. That rule works almost everywhere, which is exactly the problem: it works so often that students stop questioning it, and that's precisely where a small set of fixed expressions quietly breaks the pattern.</p>
<ul>
<li>You've memorized "to + base verb" as a fixed rule, so seeing "to + verb-ing" feels grammatically wrong even when it's correct</li>
<li>You don't recognize which expressions use "to" as a preposition, because they aren't taught as a separate category</li>
<li>You rely on how the sentence "sounds," and years of exposure to "to + base verb" makes the wrong form sound more natural</li>
<li>You've never seen the fixed list of "to-as-preposition" expressions grouped together in one place</li>
<li>I've seen strong students, ones who never make a tense error, lose marks here — not from lack of grammar knowledge, but because nobody ever told them "to" isn't always the same "to"</li>
</ul>
<p>But here's what most people get wrong: they try to memorize individual example sentences instead of learning the fixed list of expressions where "to" behaves as a preposition.</p>

<h2 id="common-mistakes">3 Mistakes That Cost the Most Marks</h2>

<p><strong>1. Treating every "to" as an infinitive marker.</strong><br>Students default to "to + base verb" everywhere, including after expressions like "look forward to" and "object to," where "to" is actually a preposition requiring a gerund.</p>

<p><strong>2. Confusing "used to" (habit) with "be used to" (accustomed to).</strong><br>"I used to smoke" uses "to" as an infinitive marker before a base verb, describing a past habit. "I am used to smoking" uses "to" as a preposition, describing familiarity — these look almost identical but follow completely different rules.</p>

<p><strong>3. Assuming a verb right after "to" always signals the infinitive.</strong><br>Students see "to" immediately followed by a verb and assume it must be the base form, without checking whether the surrounding expression is one of the fixed "to-as-preposition" phrases.</p>

