<h2 id="the-solution">The Complete "To" Trap System, Step by Step</h2>
<p>Instead of guessing case by case, learn the fixed list of expressions where "to" acts as a preposition. If the expression is on this list, "to" is always followed by a gerund — no exceptions.</p>

<div class="table-responsive">
<table class="exam-table">
<tr><th>Expression</th><th>"To" Role</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>look forward to</td><td>Preposition</td><td>I look forward to hearing from you.</td></tr>
<tr><td>object to</td><td>Preposition</td><td>He objected to being ignored.</td></tr>
<tr><td>be used to / get used to</td><td>Preposition</td><td>She is used to working nights.</td></tr>
<tr><td>be accustomed to</td><td>Preposition</td><td>They are accustomed to waiting.</td></tr>
<tr><td>confess to</td><td>Preposition</td><td>He confessed to cheating in the exam.</td></tr>
<tr><td>be committed to / dedicated to</td><td>Preposition</td><td>She is committed to improving her English.</td></tr>
<tr><td>with a view to</td><td>Preposition</td><td>He studied hard with a view to passing the exam.</td></tr>
<tr><td>want to, decide to, plan to</td><td>Infinitive marker</td><td>I want to submit the form.</td></tr>
</table>
</div>

<h3>Step 1: Learn the Fixed List First</h3>
<p>Memorize the prepositional "to" expressions as one connected group, not as separate exceptions you rediscover each time.</p>
<div class="rule-example-box">
<div class="example-wrong">❌ <em>I am looking forward to attend the workshop.</em></div>
<div class="example-correct">✅ <em>I am looking forward to <strong>attending</strong> the workshop.</em> ("look forward to" is a fixed prepositional expression)</div>
</div>

<h3>Step 2: Apply the Noun-Swap Test</h3>
<p>Replace the verb after "to" with a simple noun. If the sentence still makes sense, "to" is a preposition, and a gerund is required.</p>
<div class="rule-example-box">
<div class="example-wrong">❌ <em>He objected to pay the fine.</em></div>
<div class="example-correct">✅ <em>He objected to <strong>paying</strong> the fine.</em> (Compare: He objected to the fine — noun fits, so gerund fits)</div>
</div>

<h3>Step 3: Separate "Used To" From "Be/Get Used To"</h3>
<p>This exact pair is one of the most frequently tested patterns in SSC CGL and Bank PO exams — treat them as two entirely different structures.</p>

<div class="table-responsive">
<table class="exam-table">
<tr><th>Structure</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Followed By</th></tr>
<tr><td>used to + base verb</td><td>Past habit, no longer true</td><td>Base verb (e.g. used to play)</td></tr>
<tr><td>be used to + gerund</td><td>Familiar/accustomed to something</td><td>Gerund (e.g. is used to playing)</td></tr>
<tr><td>get used to + gerund</td><td>Becoming accustomed to something</td><td>Gerund (e.g. getting used to living)</td></tr>
</table>
</div>

<h3>Step 4: Watch for the Trap in Longer Sentences</h3>
<p>In error-spotting questions, the "to" trap often hides in a subordinate clause, not the main sentence.</p>
<div class="rule-example-box">
<div class="example-wrong">❌ <em>The team, committed to improve their results, worked overtime.</em></div>
<div class="example-correct">✅ <em>The team, committed to <strong>improving</strong> their results, worked overtime.</em> ("committed to" is a fixed prepositional expression)</div>
</div>

<h3>Step 5: Confirm With the Default Rule</h3>
<p>If the expression is not on the fixed prepositional list, "to" is almost certainly an infinitive marker and takes the base verb — don't overcorrect by adding "-ing" everywhere.</p>
<div class="rule-example-box">
<div class="example-wrong">❌ <em>She decided to accepting the offer.</em></div>
<div class="example-correct">✅ <em>She decided <strong>to accept</strong> the offer.</em> ("decide to" is a standard infinitive verb, not prepositional)</div>
</div>

<h3>Step 6: Drill Error-Spotting Sentences With Both Patterns Mixed</h3>
<p>Exams deliberately mix infinitive "to" and prepositional "to" in the same paper to test whether you're applying the rule mechanically or actually checking each expression.</p>

