<h2 id="case-study">A Quick Turnaround Story</h2>
<p>One of my SSC CGL batch students kept losing marks on sentences using "look forward to" and "committed to," convinced her grammar was solid because she never made a single tense mistake. Once she learned the fixed list of prepositional "to" expressions and started applying the noun-swap test, her accuracy on this exact pattern went from guesswork to near-perfect within a week of daily practice — the same grammar knowledge she already had, just applied to the right nine or ten expressions instead of assumed to work everywhere.</p>

<h2 id="expert-tips">Expert Tips From the Classroom</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keep the prepositional "to" list on one revision card.</strong> There are only about ten common expressions — review them the morning of your exam.</li>
<li><strong>Never trust how the sentence sounds.</strong> Years of using "to + base verb" makes the correct gerund form sound wrong at first.</li>
<li><strong>Use the noun-swap test as your final check</strong> whenever you're unsure whether "to" is prepositional or an infinitive marker.</li>
<li><strong>Pay special attention to "used to" vs "be used to"</strong> — this specific pair appears repeatedly across SSC CGL and Bank PO papers.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="conclusion">The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The "to" trap stops being confusing the moment you stop assuming every "to" behaves the same way. Learn the fixed list of prepositional "to" expressions, apply the noun-swap test when unsure, and default to the infinitive rule everywhere else — that combination is exactly what SSC CGL and Bank PO exams test on this pattern every single year.</p>

