<div class="faq-section-block">
<h3>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)</h3>
<div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-question">Q1. How do I know if "to" is a preposition or an infinitive marker?</div><div class="faq-answer">Check whether the expression before "to" is on the fixed prepositional list (look forward to, object to, be used to, committed to, and similar). If it is, "to" is a preposition and needs a gerund. Otherwise, it's usually an infinitive marker followed by a base verb.</div></div>
<div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-question">Q2. What's the difference between "used to" and "be used to"?</div><div class="faq-answer">"Used to" describes a past habit that no longer happens and is followed by a base verb ("I used to smoke"). "Be used to" or "get used to" means being accustomed to something and is followed by a gerund ("I am used to smoking").</div></div>
<div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-question">Q3. Is there a quick test to check if "to" needs a gerund?</div><div class="faq-answer">Yes — try replacing the verb after "to" with a simple noun. If the sentence still makes sense, "to" is a preposition and a gerund is correct.</div></div>
<div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-question">Q4. Does this "to" trap appear in cloze tests as well as error spotting?</div><div class="faq-answer">Yes, both. Cloze tests often leave a blank right after a prepositional "to" expression, expecting the gerund form rather than the base verb.</div></div>
<div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-question">Q5. How many prepositional "to" expressions should I memorize?</div><div class="faq-answer">Around ten common ones cover most exam questions: look forward to, object to, be/get used to, be accustomed to, confess to, be committed to, be dedicated to, and with a view to.</div></div>
</div>

